---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/ui/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  "name": "About",
  "url": `${SITE.url}/about/`,
  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": SITE.founderName,
    "jobTitle": "Founder",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": SITE.legalName },
  },
};
---

<Layout
  title={`About — ${SITE.founderName} | ${SITE.name}`}
  description="Three years as a Google Ads Account Strategist inside Google. Why that job doesn't look like what most agencies do, and why I left to fix that gap directly."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} /></div>

  <section class="section about-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>I spent three years telling advertisers what their agencies should have told them already.</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section about-essay">
    <div class="container about-essay-inner">
      <p class="lede">
        My name's {SITE.founderName}. Before {SITE.name}, I was a Google Ads Account Strategist
        at Google — not an agency employee, an actual Google employee, sitting inside the
        systems that decide who wins an auction and why. This page is longer than most "about"
        pages because that job is genuinely misunderstood, and it explains most of how I
        run accounts now.
      </p>

      <h2>What an Account Strategist actually does</h2>
      <p>
        It's not sales, even though the title sounds like it. Google assigns Account
        Strategists to advertisers who spend enough to warrant direct support — and the job is
        to sit on calls with those advertisers (and often their agencies) and go through the
        account the way an internal reviewer would: campaign structure, bidding strategy,
        conversion tracking, creative testing. You're looking at the same dashboards the
        advertiser sees, plus a few Google doesn't expose publicly, and telling them, plainly,
        what's costing them money and why.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strange part of the job is how often the answer had nothing to do with Google's
        algorithm and everything to do with how the account was built. An account with one
        campaign covering four different services, no negative keywords, and a conversion
        action that fired on every page load — that account was never going to perform well,
        no matter how the auction behaved that week. I said a version of that sentence,
        or something close to it, in probably four hundred calls over three years.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I left</h2>
      <p>
        Two things sat next to each other for a long time before I did anything about them.
        First: the advertisers I spoke with directly — the ones large enough to get an Account
        Strategist — got noticeably better results almost immediately, just from someone
        walking through the account with actual rigor. Second: almost none of that rigor was
        reaching the businesses too small to ever get that kind of attention from Google
        itself. A clinic in Indore or a plumber in Thane was never going to get a call from
        someone like me — they were going to get whichever agency answered the phone first,
        and hope.
      </p>
      <p>
        I left to close that specific gap, not a general one. Not "digital marketing is broken"
        — a lot of agencies do solid work. Specifically: the standard of account structure and
        tracking rigor that Google applies internally to its biggest advertisers almost never
        reaches a business spending ₹30,000 a month. There's no reason it can't. It just needs
        someone willing to run small accounts with that same discipline instead of treating
        them as too small to bother.
      </p>

      <h2>What agencies get wrong, most often</h2>
      <p>
        Not effort — most agencies I reviewed as an Account Strategist were trying. The
        recurring mistake was structural: one campaign built to "cover" a business instead of
        being split by what the business actually sells and how urgently people need it.
        Conversion tracking set up once at launch and never checked again, so nobody notices
        when it silently breaks — which it does, more often than you'd think. Monthly reports
        built to look complete rather than to be useful, with screenshots instead of the
        actual search terms that spent the budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that is dishonesty. It's usually an account manager handling twenty accounts
        who genuinely doesn't have the hours to do it properly. The fix isn't a better sales
        pitch — it's fewer accounts per person and more time spent on structure before a single
        rupee goes toward bids.
      </p>

      <h2>Why "Structure"</h2>
      <p>
        Because it's the first thing I check, the thing most other problems trace back to, and
        the one part of this work that's genuinely visible — you can look at an account tree
        and see whether it makes sense, the same way you can look at a building's frame before
        it's finished and tell whether it'll hold. Everything else — bids, budgets, creative —
        is easier to get right once the structure underneath it is sound.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing I managed before this existed can be shown here — it was all under NDA, inside
        Google's systems, tied to advertisers who never knew my name. So instead of a case
        study, there's a free audit: send over your account (or point us at your competitors,
        if you're not running ads yet) and you'll get the same written findings I used to give
        advertisers directly. That's the actual pitch. Everything else on this site explains
        how the work gets done after that.
      </p>

      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary about-cta">Get the free account audit</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="rule"></div>

  <section class="section about-creds tint">
    <div class="container about-creds-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Background</h3>
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>3 years as a Google Ads Account Strategist at Google</li>
          <li>Google Ads certified</li>
          <li>Meta Blueprint certified</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What this doesn't include</h3>
        <ul class="about-list about-list-muted">
          <li>An official Google Partner badge — that's an agency-level award, not yet earned, and won't appear here until it genuinely is</li>
          <li>Client logos or case studies from Google — all under NDA, none of it is mine to show</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .about-hero h1 { margin-top: 0.6rem; max-width: 28ch; }

  .about-essay-inner { max-width: 42rem; }
  .about-essay-inner .lede { max-width: none; font-size: var(--fs-lg); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .about-essay-inner h2 { margin-block: 2.25rem 0.85rem; font-size: var(--fs-h2); }
  .about-essay-inner p { color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1.1rem; max-width: none; }
  .about-cta { margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .about-creds-grid { display: grid; gap: 2rem; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) {
    .about-creds-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 4rem; }
  }
  .about-creds-grid h3 { font-size: var(--fs-sm); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .about-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.65rem; }
  .about-list li { font-size: var(--fs-sm); padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--ink); max-width: 46ch; }
  .about-list-muted li { border-left-color: var(--rule); color: var(--muted); }
</style>
